{
  "dom_id": "step-q73777",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+1)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x+2$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+8x+7$\n\nIII. $h(x)=-{x}^{2}+x$",
  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+1)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-1$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-1$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-1$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1-3+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-1$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+8(-1)+7 \\\\  & =1-8+7 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-1$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
